-- src/core/dst_attribution_table.ail
--
-- WI-A5, under ADR-001 D4 clause 3: the site-to-hook attribution table and the
-- validation a profile runs against it at load.
--
-- This module owns the TABLE and the RULES. It does not own the profile
-- definition or the manifest — those are WI-A10's, and they consume this. A
-- profile records the table's IDENTITY, the pair `(source revision, content
-- hash)`, never a copy: one source of truth, no divergent embedded copies, and
-- the stated consequence that correcting one row re-issues every profile that
-- references the table.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- What the table is for, in one paragraph
-- ============================================================================
--
-- D4 defines "profile-reachable" by installation, not by reachability analysis:
-- a site is reachable if it is (1) in the core driver, (2) in a module the
-- profile installs, or (3) a core site ATTRIBUTED to an installed hook by this
-- table. Clause 3 exists because some core sites only execute when a particular
-- extension is installed, and without a way to say so they default to clause 1
-- and every profile must route them — including profiles for which they are
-- dead. The absence of `test_dummy` is exactly the fact that removes
-- `src/core/ext/runtime.ail:199` from a profile's reachable set.
--
-- The mechanical rule, with the empty-intersection case answered:
--
--   a core effect site with a VALID row is profile-reachable exactly when that
--   row's hook set intersects the profile's installed hooks; a site with NO
--   valid row is an unconditional core site under clause 1.
--
-- An empty intersection means the site is genuinely unreachable in that
-- profile. Only a MISSING or INVALID row falls back to clause 1.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- Why this is an attribution table and not a semantic test
-- ============================================================================
--
-- The rejected reading was "guarded solely by an installed hook's identity". A
-- site guarded by hook identity AND something else fails "solely", falls
-- through unclassified, and is then not required to be routed — fail-open, in
-- the clause that exists to close exactly that hole. The ADR's worked example is
-- row 2 below: a real mixed guard in the tool phase. Attribution is a claim
-- about which hook can CAUSE the site to execute, not a claim that the hook is
-- the only condition, so additional guards never remove a site from an
-- attributed hook's scope.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- The correctness condition, and the exception it forces
-- ============================================================================
--
-- An attribution asserts that installation of at least one attributed hook is a
-- NECESSARY condition of the site executing. Necessity is what makes removal
-- from clause 1 safe; sufficiency is what mixed guards break.
--
-- Nothing in this module checks that. The validators below are schema,
-- staleness, referential-integrity and completeness checks, and a well-formed
-- but FALSE attribution passes every one of them. Necessity is manually
-- reviewed and each row records a named reviewer. **This is a stated exception
-- to the automated-gate promise**, not an oversight, and it stands until the
-- interprocedural necessity validator exists — which D4 assigns as its own
-- future obligation and this item deliberately does not build. One mechanical
-- check was proposed and WITHDRAWN rather than patched (syntactic dominance,
-- which fails on this table's own first row: the attributed site is inside
-- `emit_dummy_hook`, which contains no guard at all — the five `is_test_dummy`
-- guards dominate the five CALLS to it, and relating them follows a call edge).
-- A check that fails its own worked example is worse than none.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- Three things measured at HEAD that the ADR's own text no longer describes
-- ============================================================================
--
-- 1. **Row 2's line moved.** D4 cites `src/core/tool_phase.ail:222` for the
--    mixed-guard example. WI-A12 rewrote that file; the guard is now at `:317`
--    and the effectful call it guards is `:318`. The ADR's prose anchor is
--    stale, the example itself is intact. (This note said `:286`/`:287` from
--    WI-A12 until WI-D20, which moved the pair twice over — the offsets are
--    re-stated rather than re-dated because the claim is about HEAD, not about
--    what a past item did; plan rule S15 governs the latter and not this.)
--
-- 2. **The core clock inventory is no longer "four driver sites".** D4's table
--    says four; at HEAD there are SIX routed core sites and ONE ambient one.
--    WI-A12 added `tool_phase.ail:344` (the fifth) and created two ambient
--    sites that did not exist when D4's table was written. WI-C5 then widened
--    `ExtPorts.clock_now` to thread the world token, which let
--    `session.ext_ports_of` route its extension seam through `Ports.clock_now`
--    like every other: that added `session.ail:881` as the SIXTH routed site
--    and RETIRED the ambient `session.ail:878` entirely — the function that
--    carried it (`ext_unrouted_clock`) no longer exists. The one ambient site
--    left is `stub_step.ail:203`, the live adapter, which is ambient by design.
--    See `unconditional_core_sites` for the measured set. This is why the
--    completeness check below takes the discovered set as an ARGUMENT and does
--    not transcribe a number from the ADR.
--
-- 3. **`stub_step.ail:203` is core, unattributed, and UNROUTED.** Falling it back
--    to clause 1 makes it unconditional core, which is right — but a profile
--    that then claims to route its whole unconditional set would be claiming
--    something false. (`session.ail:878` was the OTHER member of this note
--    until WI-C5 routed it; the rule is unchanged, the membership shrank.) So the declaration carries `routed` per site and an
--    unrouted entry is a declared, visible gap (plan S2: prefer the un-routed
--    option that fails loudly) rather than a silent one. Whether such a profile
--    is conformant is D4's all-or-nothing rule to apply, and WI-A10's to apply
--    it; this module's job is to make the fact impossible to miss.
--
-- ============================================================================
-- The trap that would have made the intersection rule fail open
-- ============================================================================
--
-- Rows name BASE hook ids (`test_dummy`, `scratchpad`). Installed hooks do not
-- carry those ids: `parse_tokens` (`src/core/ext/registry_generated.ail:52-64`)
-- stamps every instance as `"${name}#${idx}"`, so a runtime registry holds
-- `scratchpad#5`. String equality between a row's `test_dummy` and an installed
-- `test_dummy#0` finds NOTHING, the intersection comes back empty, and the
-- empty-intersection rule then declares the site genuinely unreachable and
-- drops it from the profile — the precise inversion of what the table is for,
-- and silent. `hook_id_matches` mirrors the runtime's own two predicates
-- (`ext/runtime.ail:196`, `tool_phase.ail:175`) and
-- `test_instance_suffixed_ids_intersect` is what holds it there.

module src/core/dst_attribution_table

import std/list as List (length)
import std/option (Option, Some, None)
import std/result (Result, Ok, Err)
import std/string (startsWith)
import std/crypto (sha256Hex)

-- The version of the RULES in this module. Changing what attribution means
-- changes it for every profile, so it is versioned on its own footing and
-- WI-A10's manifest records it alongside the table identity.
export pure func attribution_rules_version() -> string { "attribution-rules/1" }

-- One source location. Line numbers are part of the binding: D4 says a source
-- change that adds, moves, or re-guards a core effect site invalidates the
-- table, and the source revision below is what enforces that.
export type SiteRef = {
  file: string,
  line: int
}

export pure func site_key(s: SiteRef) -> string { "${s.file}:${show(s.line)}" }

-- One attribution row. D4: the site, its effect, the KNOWN hook ids it is
-- attributed to, and the named reviewer who accepted the attribution.
export type AttributionRow = {
  site: SiteRef,
  effect: string,
  hook_ids: [string],
  reviewer: string,
  note: string
}

-- A core effect site that is NOT attributed to any hook — clause 1,
-- unconditional core. Declared explicitly rather than left to the fallback,
-- because the completeness check below is exactly the question "did anything
-- discovered end up in neither list", and a fallback cannot answer it.
--
-- `routed` records whether the site actually reaches the world protocol. See
-- header note 3: an unrouted entry is a declared gap, not a passing one.
export type CoreSite = {
  site: SiteRef,
  effect: string,
  routed: bool,
  note: string
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The artifact
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The source revision this table is bound to. A profile referencing the table
-- must be loaded against this revision or the binding is stale and validation
-- fails closed. This is the commit A5 measured the rows against.
export pure func table_source_revision() -> string {
  "c0fbf10"
}

-- The two initial rows.
--
-- Row 1 is D4's own example of clause 3 earning its keep: `emit_dummy_hook`
-- reads the clock ambiently and is reached only through the five
-- `is_test_dummy` guards in `ext/runtime.ail`. No checked-in configuration
-- installs `test_dummy`, so in every one of them this row's hook set intersects
-- the installed set emptily and the site is correctly dropped. That is the
-- whole point: before the table existed the site fell back to clause 1 and
-- every profile owed it.
--
-- Row 2 is D4's worked example of why clause 3 is an attribution table rather
-- than a semantic test. The guard is MIXED —
-- `is_scratchpad_tool_name(envelope.tool) && scratchpad_extension_active(rt)` —
-- so it is not "solely" hook identity and a semantic test would drop it
-- unclassified. Attribution states only that installing `scratchpad` is
-- necessary for the call to execute, which is true and is all clause 3 needs.
--
-- **Row 2's necessity cannot be established mechanically here, and that is D4's
-- known gap rather than a blocker.** The guard's second conjunct calls
-- `scratchpad_extension_active(rt)`, which folds `rt.registry.hooks` in a
-- separate function (`tool_phase.ail:168-177`); relating it to the hook id
-- follows a call edge, which is the interprocedural reasoning clause 3 exists
-- to avoid. The row is recorded with its named reviewer and the gap is stated.
export pure func attribution_rows() -> [AttributionRow] {
  [
    {
      site: { file: "src/core/ext/runtime.ail", line: 199 },
      effect: "Clock",
      hook_ids: ["test_dummy"],
      reviewer: "arni.westh",
      note: "ambient now() inside emit_dummy_hook (:187-195), reached only via the five is_test_dummy guards in this file; installing test_dummy is necessary for it to execute"
    },
    {
      site: { file: "src/core/tool_phase.ail", line: 318 },
      effect: "Process",
      hook_ids: ["scratchpad"],
      reviewer: "arni.westh",
      note: "exec_scratchpad_cell_ws under the MIXED guard at :317; necessity rests on the named reviewer because the second conjunct is interprocedural (D4's stated gap)"
    }
  ]
}

-- The explicit unconditional-core set. Measured at HEAD, not transcribed: see
-- header note 2 for why the ADR's "four driver sites" no longer describes it.
--
-- Scope is the CLOCK effect in core, which is the inventory D4 enumerates and
-- the one A12's routing claim is made over. Sites in installed packages are
-- clause 2 and are not this table's business; `packages/motoko-ext-compose`'s
-- eight reads are reachable by installation, not by attribution.
export pure func unconditional_core_sites() -> [CoreSite] {
  [
    { site: { file: "src/core/session.ail", line: 1111 }, effect: "Clock", routed: true,
      note: "ext_ports_of's extension clock seam — the SIXTH routed core site, added by WI-C5. It replaced the ambient `session.ail:878` rather than joining it: widening ExtPorts.clock_now to take the world token removed the shape obstacle that plan rule S2 had accommodated, so the seam routes through Ports.clock_now like every other" },
    { site: { file: "src/core/session.ail", line: 1370 }, effect: "Clock", routed: true,
      note: "ports.clock_now — routed by WI-A12" },
    { site: { file: "src/core/session.ail", line: 1476 }, effect: "Clock", routed: true,
      note: "ports.clock_now — routed by WI-A12" },
    { site: { file: "src/core/session.ail", line: 2921 }, effect: "Clock", routed: true,
      note: "provider.ports.clock_now — routed by WI-A12" },
    { site: { file: "src/core/session.ail", line: 3031 }, effect: "Clock", routed: true,
      note: "provider.ports.clock_now — routed by WI-A12" },
    { site: { file: "src/core/tool_phase.ail", line: 413 }, effect: "Clock", routed: true,
      note: "ports.clock_now — the FIFTH routed core site; D4's table says four and predates it. WI-D20 moved it 373 -> 413 and CHANGED ITS ARGUMENT: it read ports.clock_now(world) and now reads ports.clock_now(handle_world), because a DELEGATING on_tool_handle may have advanced the world before this native dispatch begins and starting from `world` would discard what it did. Same site, same effect, same routing, different starting world — recorded here because the other two tool_phase anchors this item moved are byte-identical drift and this one is not" },
    { site: { file: "src/core/test/stub_step.ail", line: 203 }, effect: "Clock", routed: false,
      note: "live_ports' clock adapter — the one place the driver reads a real clock, by design; a deterministic profile uses the scripted adapter and never reaches it" }
  ]
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Identity: (source revision, content hash)
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure func join_ids(xs: [string]) -> string {
  match xs {
    [] => "",
    x :: [] => x,
    x :: rest => "${x}|${join_ids(rest)}"
  }
}

pure func canonical_rows(rs: [AttributionRow]) -> string {
  match rs {
    [] => "",
    r :: rest =>
      "row|${site_key(r.site)}|${r.effect}|${join_ids(r.hook_ids)}|${r.reviewer}\n${canonical_rows(rest)}"
  }
}

pure func routed_tag(b: bool) -> string { if b then "R" else "U" }

pure func canonical_core(cs: [CoreSite]) -> string {
  match cs {
    [] => "",
    c :: rest =>
      "core|${site_key(c.site)}|${c.effect}|${routed_tag(c.routed)}\n${canonical_core(rest)}"
  }
}

-- The content hash covers the rows, the unconditional-core declaration, and the
-- rules version — everything a profile's conformance depends on. The `note`
-- fields are deliberately EXCLUDED: they are prose for a reader, and including
-- them would re-issue every referring profile over a typo fix. The reviewer IS
-- included, because changing who accepted an attribution changes the only thing
-- standing behind its necessity.
export pure func table_content_hash() -> string {
  let payload = "${attribution_rules_version()}\n${canonical_rows(attribution_rows())}${canonical_core(unconditional_core_sites())}";
  "sha256:${sha256Hex(payload)}"
}

export type TableIdentity = {
  source_revision: string,
  content_hash: string
}

-- What a profile records. Not a copy of the table.
export pure func table_identity() -> TableIdentity {
  { source_revision: table_source_revision(), content_hash: table_content_hash() }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Known hook ids
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- The extension names `resolve` accepts (`src/core/ext/registry_generated.ail:28-44`).
-- D4: reject UNKNOWN hook ids; PERMIT known-but-uninstalled ones. An earlier
-- revision rejected uninstalled ids, which defeats the table's whole purpose —
-- under that rule row 1 could not exist in any checked-in configuration, the
-- site defaulted to unconditional core, and the driver obligation was five.
export pure func known_hook_ids() -> [string] {
  ["test_dummy", "omnigraph", "context_mode", "mcp", "exa_search", "ailang_docs",
   "compose", "a2a", "decision_framework", "microrag", "compaction_ai",
   "scratchpad", "compaction_structural", "empty_stop_guard", "progress_contract_guard"]
}

pure func contains_str(xs: [string], target: string) -> bool {
  match xs {
    [] => false,
    x :: rest => if x == target then true else contains_str(rest, target)
  }
}

-- An installed hook id matches a row's base id when it IS that id or is one of
-- its `#idx` instances. See the header: plain equality here is a silent
-- fail-open that empties every intersection.
export pure func hook_id_matches(installed_id: string, base_id: string) -> bool {
  installed_id == base_id || startsWith(installed_id, "${base_id}#")
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rejections
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

-- Typed rather than stringly, so WI-A10 can render the load-time rejection
-- without re-deriving the rule and a fixture can assert WHICH rule fired.
export type AttributionRejection
  = UnknownHookId(string, string)
  | StaleSourceRevision(string, string)
  | MalformedRow(string, string)
  | DuplicateSite(string)
  | SiteUnaccounted(string, string)

export pure func rejection_rule(r: AttributionRejection) -> string {
  match r {
    UnknownHookId(_, _) => "unknown-hook-id",
    StaleSourceRevision(_, _) => "stale-source-revision",
    MalformedRow(_, _) => "malformed-row",
    DuplicateSite(_) => "duplicate-site",
    SiteUnaccounted(_, _) => "site-unaccounted"
  }
}

export pure func rejection_message(r: AttributionRejection) -> string {
  match r {
    UnknownHookId(s, h) =>
      "[unknown-hook-id] row for '${s}' names hook id '${h}', which no extension registers; known-but-uninstalled is permitted, unknown is not (D4 clause 3)",
    StaleSourceRevision(want, got) =>
      "[stale-source-revision] the attribution table is bound to source revision '${want}' but the profile is loading against '${got}'; a source change can add, move, or re-guard an attributed site (D4 clause 3)",
    MalformedRow(s, why) =>
      "[malformed-row] row for '${s}' is malformed: ${why} (D4 clause 3)",
    DuplicateSite(s) =>
      "[duplicate-site] site '${s}' is attributed more than once; one row per core effect site (D4 clause 3)",
    SiteUnaccounted(s, e) =>
      "[site-unaccounted] discovered core effect site '${s}' (${e}) appears in neither the attribution rows nor the unconditional-core set; the profile's routing claim does not cover it (D4 clause 3)"
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Validation. Schema, staleness, referential integrity, completeness.
-- None of these tests the correctness condition — see the header.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure func unknown_ids(site: string, ids: [string], known: [string]) -> [AttributionRejection] {
  match ids {
    [] => [],
    h :: rest => {
      let here = if contains_str(known, h) then [] else [UnknownHookId(site, h)];
      here ++ unknown_ids(site, rest, known)
    }
  }
}

-- A row must name a file, a positive line, at least one hook, and a reviewer.
-- The reviewer check is not bureaucracy: it is the ONLY thing standing behind
-- necessity, so a row without one has no correctness claim at all.
pure func row_shape(r: AttributionRow) -> [AttributionRejection] {
  let s = site_key(r.site);
  (if r.site.file == "" then [MalformedRow(s, "empty file")] else [])
    ++ (if r.site.line <= 0 then [MalformedRow(s, "line must be positive")] else [])
    ++ (if r.effect == "" then [MalformedRow(s, "empty effect")] else [])
    ++ (if List.length(r.hook_ids) == 0 then
          [MalformedRow(s, "no attributed hooks; a row attributing to nothing is an unconditional-core site, declare it there")]
        else [])
    ++ (if r.reviewer == "" then
          [MalformedRow(s, "no named reviewer; necessity is manually reviewed and the reviewer is the only thing standing behind it")]
        else [])
}

pure func seen_site(keys: [string], k: string) -> bool { contains_str(keys, k) }

pure func validate_rows_rec(seen: [string], rs: [AttributionRow], known: [string]) -> [AttributionRejection] {
  match rs {
    [] => [],
    r :: rest => {
      let k = site_key(r.site);
      let dup = if seen_site(seen, k) then [DuplicateSite(k)] else [];
      dup ++ row_shape(r) ++ unknown_ids(k, r.hook_ids, known)
        ++ validate_rows_rec(k :: seen, rest, known)
    }
  }
}

-- Every row-level check, over the whole table. Returns ALL violations rather
-- than the first, so a fixture asserting one rule cannot pass because another
-- fired ahead of it.
export pure func validate_rows(rs: [AttributionRow], known: [string]) -> [AttributionRejection] {
  validate_rows_rec([], rs, known)
}

-- The staleness check. A profile loading against a different revision than the
-- table was bound to fails closed: D4 says a source change that adds, moves or
-- re-guards a site invalidates the table, and nothing here can tell which kind
-- of change a revision bump was.
export pure func validate_binding(bound_to: string, loading_against: string) -> [AttributionRejection] {
  if bound_to == loading_against then [] else [StaleSourceRevision(bound_to, loading_against)]
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Producer-side completeness — the check that row shape cannot supply
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure func row_covers(rs: [AttributionRow], k: string) -> bool {
  match rs {
    [] => false,
    r :: rest => if site_key(r.site) == k then true else row_covers(rest, k)
  }
}

pure func core_covers(cs: [CoreSite], k: string) -> bool {
  match cs {
    [] => false,
    c :: rest => if site_key(c.site) == k then true else core_covers(rest, k)
  }
}

-- Every site an inventory DISCOVERED must appear in the rows or in the
-- unconditional-core set. A table that silently omits a discovered site is
-- syntactically valid, passes every check above, and leaves the profile
-- claiming routing completeness over a site nobody classified. That is the
-- fail-open D4 clause 3 exists to close, and no row-shape check can see it —
-- the defect is in what is ABSENT.
--
-- The discovered set is an ARGUMENT, never a constant in this module. It comes
-- from an inventory run against the source, and hardcoding it here would make
-- this check agree with itself by construction.
export pure func validate_completeness(
  discovered: [CoreSite],
  rs: [AttributionRow],
  core: [CoreSite]
) -> [AttributionRejection] {
  match discovered {
    [] => [],
    d :: rest => {
      let k = site_key(d.site);
      let here =
        if row_covers(rs, k) || core_covers(core, k) then []
        else [SiteUnaccounted(k, d.effect)];
      here ++ validate_completeness(rest, rs, core)
    }
  }
}

-- The whole load-time gate: schema, staleness, referential integrity, and
-- completeness, in one call. WI-A10 calls this.
export pure func validate_at_load(
  loading_against: string,
  discovered: [CoreSite]
) -> [AttributionRejection] {
  validate_binding(table_source_revision(), loading_against)
    ++ validate_rows(attribution_rows(), known_hook_ids())
    ++ validate_completeness(discovered, attribution_rows(), unconditional_core_sites())
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- The reachable set: the empty-intersection rule
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure func any_installed(installed: [string], base_ids: [string]) -> bool {
  match base_ids {
    [] => false,
    b :: rest =>
      if any_matches(installed, b) then true else any_installed(installed, rest)
  }
}

pure func any_matches(installed: [string], base: string) -> bool {
  match installed {
    [] => false,
    i :: rest => if hook_id_matches(i, base) then true else any_matches(rest, base)
  }
}

-- Is an ATTRIBUTED site reachable in a profile installing these hooks?
--
-- Exactly when the row's hook set intersects the installed set. An empty
-- intersection means the site is genuinely unreachable in that profile — which
-- is the fact the table exists to express, NOT a fallback to clause 1. Only a
-- missing or invalid row falls back.
export pure func row_is_reachable(r: AttributionRow, installed: [string]) -> bool {
  any_installed(installed, r.hook_ids)
}

-- The core effect sites a profile must route: every unconditional-core site,
-- plus every attributed site whose hook set intersects what it installs.
export pure func reachable_core_sites(installed: [string]) -> [CoreSite] {
  unconditional_core_sites() ++ reachable_attributed(attribution_rows(), installed)
}

pure func reachable_attributed(rs: [AttributionRow], installed: [string]) -> [CoreSite] {
  match rs {
    [] => [],
    r :: rest => {
      let here =
        if row_is_reachable(r, installed) then
          [{ site: r.site, effect: r.effect, routed: false, note: "attributed to ${join_ids(r.hook_ids)}" }]
        else [];
      here ++ reachable_attributed(rest, installed)
    }
  }
}

-- A site with no valid row is unconditional core. Stated as a function because
-- it is the clause-1 fallback and a fixture asserts it directly.
export pure func classify_site(k: string, installed: [string]) -> string {
  match find_row(attribution_rows(), k) {
    None => "unconditional-core",
    Some(r) => if row_is_reachable(r, installed) then "attributed-reachable" else "attributed-unreachable"
  }
}

pure func find_row(rs: [AttributionRow], k: string) -> Option[AttributionRow] {
  match rs {
    [] => None,
    r :: rest => if site_key(r.site) == k then Some(r) else find_row(rest, k)
  }
}

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Tests
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

pure func has_rule(rs: [AttributionRejection], want: string) -> bool {
  match rs {
    [] => false,
    r :: rest => if rejection_rule(r) == want then true else has_rule(rest, want)
  }
}

pure func test_table_at_head_validates() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    List.length(validate_rows(attribution_rows(), known_hook_ids())) == 0
      && List.length(validate_binding(table_source_revision(), table_source_revision())) == 0
  }

pure func test_unknown_hook_id_is_rejected() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let bad: [AttributionRow] = [{
      site: { file: "src/core/rpc.ail", line: 200 },
      effect: "SharedMem",
      hook_ids: ["scratchpadd"],
      reviewer: "arni.westh",
      note: "typo"
    }];
    has_rule(validate_rows(bad, known_hook_ids()), "unknown-hook-id")
  }

-- The rule that an earlier ADR revision had backwards. `test_dummy` is in NO
-- checked-in configuration, and the row naming it must still be legal — that
-- absence is exactly what removes the site from those profiles.
pure func test_known_but_uninstalled_is_permitted() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    List.length(validate_rows(attribution_rows(), known_hook_ids())) == 0
      && classify_site("src/core/ext/runtime.ail:199", ["compaction_ai#0"]) == "attributed-unreachable"
  }

pure func test_stale_binding_is_rejected() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    has_rule(validate_binding("5ad3433", "deadbee"), "stale-source-revision")
  }

pure func test_malformed_rows_are_rejected() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let no_reviewer: [AttributionRow] = [{
      site: { file: "src/core/x.ail", line: 1 }, effect: "Clock",
      hook_ids: ["scratchpad"], reviewer: "", note: ""
    }];
    let no_hooks: [AttributionRow] = [{
      site: { file: "src/core/x.ail", line: 1 }, effect: "Clock",
      hook_ids: [], reviewer: "arni.westh", note: ""
    }];
    let bad_line: [AttributionRow] = [{
      site: { file: "src/core/x.ail", line: 0 }, effect: "Clock",
      hook_ids: ["scratchpad"], reviewer: "arni.westh", note: ""
    }];
    has_rule(validate_rows(no_reviewer, known_hook_ids()), "malformed-row")
      && has_rule(validate_rows(no_hooks, known_hook_ids()), "malformed-row")
      && has_rule(validate_rows(bad_line, known_hook_ids()), "malformed-row")
  }

pure func test_duplicate_site_is_rejected() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let r: AttributionRow = {
      site: { file: "src/core/x.ail", line: 9 }, effect: "Clock",
      hook_ids: ["scratchpad"], reviewer: "arni.westh", note: ""
    };
    has_rule(validate_rows([r, r], known_hook_ids()), "duplicate-site")
  }

-- The empty-intersection rule, both directions. A profile installing
-- `scratchpad` owes row 2's site; one installing only `compaction_ai` does not,
-- and that is a REMOVAL from the reachable set rather than a fallback to
-- unconditional core.
pure func test_empty_intersection_removes_the_site() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let with_sp = classify_site("src/core/tool_phase.ail:318", ["scratchpad#3"]);
    let without = classify_site("src/core/tool_phase.ail:318", ["compaction_ai#0", "context_mode#1"]);
    with_sp == "attributed-reachable" && without == "attributed-unreachable"
  }

-- The `#idx` trap. Without `hook_id_matches` every runtime intersection is
-- empty and every attributed site silently vanishes from every profile.
pure func test_instance_suffixed_ids_intersect() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    hook_id_matches("scratchpad#5", "scratchpad")
      && hook_id_matches("scratchpad", "scratchpad")
      && not hook_id_matches("scratchpad_extra", "scratchpad")
      && not hook_id_matches("compose#0", "scratchpad")
  }

-- A site with no row is unconditional core (clause 1's fallback).
pure func test_unattributed_site_is_unconditional_core() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    classify_site("src/core/session.ail:1048", ["scratchpad#0"]) == "unconditional-core"
  }

-- THE completeness test. The discovered site is real, well-formed, and in
-- neither list — every other check in this module passes on it.
pure func test_discovered_site_in_neither_list_is_rejected() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let discovered: [CoreSite] = [
      { site: { file: "src/core/session.ail", line: 1476 }, effect: "Clock", routed: true, note: "" },
      { site: { file: "src/core/ext/runtime.ail", line: 199 }, effect: "Clock", routed: false, note: "" },
      { site: { file: "src/core/omitted.ail", line: 42 }, effect: "Clock", routed: false, note: "" }
    ];
    let rs = validate_completeness(discovered, attribution_rows(), unconditional_core_sites());
    -- the row-level checks see nothing wrong: the defect is in what is ABSENT
    List.length(validate_rows(attribution_rows(), known_hook_ids())) == 0
      && List.length(rs) == 1
      && has_rule(rs, "site-unaccounted")
  }

-- The measured HEAD inventory is fully accounted for. The falsifiable half of
-- the test above: if completeness only ever fired, it would be useless.
pure func test_head_inventory_is_complete() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let discovered = unconditional_core_sites()
      ++ [{ site: { file: "src/core/ext/runtime.ail", line: 199 }, effect: "Clock", routed: false, note: "" },
          { site: { file: "src/core/tool_phase.ail", line: 318 }, effect: "Process", routed: false, note: "" }];
    List.length(validate_completeness(discovered, attribution_rows(), unconditional_core_sites())) == 0
  }

pure func test_identity_is_revision_and_hash() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let id = table_identity();
    id.source_revision == table_source_revision()
      && startsWith(id.content_hash, "sha256:")
      && id.content_hash != "sha256:"
  }

-- An unrouted unconditional-core site is DECLARED, not hidden. Header note 3:
-- `stub_step.ail:203` is core, unattributed and ambient by design, and a
-- profile claiming to route its whole unconditional set would be claiming
-- something false. This test is what keeps that fact visible.
--
-- WENT FROM 2 TO 1 AT WI-C5, and the literal is deliberately a literal: a row
-- asserting the count equals itself is green after any edit. It went red when
-- `session.ail:878` was routed, which is exactly what it is for.
pure func test_unrouted_core_sites_are_declared() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    count_unrouted(unconditional_core_sites()) == 1
  }

pure func count_unrouted(cs: [CoreSite]) -> int {
  match cs {
    [] => 0,
    c :: rest => (if c.routed then 0 else 1) + count_unrouted(rest)
  }
}

pure func test_rejection_message_names_the_rule() -> bool
  tests [((), true)]
  {
    let r = SiteUnaccounted("src/core/x.ail:1", "Clock");
    rejection_message(r) != "" && rejection_rule(r) == "site-unaccounted"
  }
